[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a0f7d72-d5b1-583b-a664-9d5b5433a29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a0f7d72-d5b1-583b-a664-9d5b5433a29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306469} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ef958fb-07c3-5eb9-9d44-c4d8aeabd281" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307621} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5e42514-14ce-5d53-a74c-ccded584eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5e42514-14ce-5d53-a74c-ccded584eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307132} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d409e8b-3085-5fc0-bba9-a24ee39f62e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69ae8d3e-d8ed-5aea-9b02-9d314bac9d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ae8d3e-d8ed-5aea-9b02-9d314bac9d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306157} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67ad5f0-d14d-54ce-b596-490a030c5184" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "621f0602-05e8-50ed-bc75-f491feb3bd4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333622" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y=3{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x},\\, && z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0+3}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+3=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b2f7f93-217d-5351-92f9-9ec524919f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b2f7f93-217d-5351-92f9-9ec524919f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306599} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e3561e-2c52-5381-a3af-6a8c4e018632" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bda07a91-6812-5ff9-9359-38ed3c4bfb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bda07a91-6812-5ff9-9359-38ed3c4bfb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306919} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d88eb1-a195-5ff7-a948-4b35d7a48ce5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afb7af7b-a7bc-565b-8fd9-0a6cfffcfdb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb7af7b-a7bc-565b-8fd9-0a6cfffcfdb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306806} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae22e312-e093-51e7-b74e-dcf5f0e914cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "650988e1-bb3f-55e1-a363-a37c84fed3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-333653" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2}={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}-2x{y}^{′}-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2},\\, && z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=2,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =1-4-3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+(-3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3b9399d-ae90-5b73-ad74-b47fa8307e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3b9399d-ae90-5b73-ad74-b47fa8307e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307110} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de5c396-310e-525e-a2bb-2b7a08c55b7b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe2b755-eef9-5d99-aeae-2726ea4e3690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe2b755-eef9-5d99-aeae-2726ea4e3690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305294} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef99d6c-ec79-5a9c-a551-0bfd40239e29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3104fbc7-8892-5672-aae4-c23b88e0757f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3104fbc7-8892-5672-aae4-c23b88e0757f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305612} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9ceaf7-2f85-5521-9fea-7b82060c3e7b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "761efed4-6b1b-5741-bad4-ad0cca8f5a8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333654" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+{y}^{3}={x}^{3},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+{y}^{3} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{3}+x{y}^{′}-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0)={y}^{′}(0)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(0)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3},\\, && z(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=1,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3} \\\\  & ={0}^{3}+0\\cdot 1-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78c726a7-66a5-5fe5-9a80-1c4529b86282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306300} {:answer/id #uuid "a7528b6a-ec3b-5c0e-ba0f-701a55e4d8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306726} {:answer/id #uuid "d44650c6-da4f-596b-a984-724aef9b05bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307121} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ae9693-34c0-50e1-9f69-789bcb474da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307231} {:answer/id #uuid "ea179e02-c445-5d4e-a67d-587f81934f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0ae9693-34c0-50e1-9f69-789bcb474da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307231} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7f0eca-a730-5c07-b583-54e35d8a27c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "862cb215-e1c8-5e4c-a0a2-cda6cb44f797" :question/math-academy-id "q-333680" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =2{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=0+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81dea119-18e3-5987-bf9b-8bac05ab7dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{71}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306392} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae9667d-f895-5cc3-a493-8eb9b0e79487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{69}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306476} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbb8fdb-1eb0-5ff9-a5fb-37ad57733adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306902} {:answer/id #uuid "bd18733f-0179-5771-873d-38d8cc2acceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306916} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f43d40-1142-5dea-a203-1a71cf717fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81dea119-18e3-5987-bf9b-8bac05ab7dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{71}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306392} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddac4028-9358-583c-89c1-83b1a59ad5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e92e1b49-1def-5e1c-b19c-ef83416fc895" :question/math-academy-id "q-333686" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6x-2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =6{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{7}{3})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6x-2y \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{7}{3}+3=\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =6{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{58}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{29}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{71}{18}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{71}{18}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca8f026-ee86-50b0-930b-37d693a6f098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{97}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305579} {:answer/id #uuid "35d1c6a8-b2e1-54f6-b378-a3364cb9f32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{109}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305668} {:answer/id #uuid "383627d1-70ce-58b0-8dd5-1154f6bea098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{121}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305689} {:answer/id #uuid "88fad9af-af24-545e-8172-f8712e211c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{105}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306461} {:answer/id #uuid "d5725f7e-ea38-5f9e-8506-e723217bda2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5725f7e-ea38-5f9e-8506-e723217bda2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307131} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7dd41f-c1eb-557e-b294-de8e0b5098dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96f9e463-c5f3-54b5-a23c-48c898be91f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333687" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-10,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(2)≈-\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(3).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & ={x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-10 \\\\  & =1-2-10 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(2,-\\frac{9}{2})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-10 \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{9}{2})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}=-\\frac{17}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-10 \\\\  & =3-(-\\frac{17}{4})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{4})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{123}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{41}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{41}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n| $3$ | $-\\frac{113}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-\\frac{113}{16}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f38c6f-56a8-5851-b1b5-7f2ed760931f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305168} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad7f15a-33fe-5dc2-ae2f-1754937da348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305872} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebe2178-d6c2-5fc3-a295-c8c4ddb2939c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306062} {:answer/id #uuid "b0489d82-6cef-5442-98bd-b9816410bd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306812} {:answer/id #uuid "d75de5f3-64a4-52e6-85ec-e7c5f318533a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ebe2178-d6c2-5fc3-a295-c8c4ddb2939c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306062} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f61863f-66d4-5938-9de5-9e7d5b9629e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cb7ed5f-cda5-5880-beb7-620f899a9264" :question/math-academy-id "q-333689" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈2\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =3{x}_{-1}-4{y}_{-1}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-8)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}-4{y}_{p}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-6)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{59}{2}+8\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 104 \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{32}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9e418b-7832-5003-9388-7d396a007f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f9e418b-7832-5003-9388-7d396a007f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305286} :answer-field/id #uuid "500ccab2-86be-544d-af77-8b1e59e58c2e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb556469-939c-5bfc-a174-5e93ad24947a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb556469-939c-5bfc-a174-5e93ad24947a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306895} :answer-field/id #uuid "831d6ef7-3e18-517c-aa89-7189db3b234b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6b42415-80aa-5fa1-b545-54cb2b76e654" :question/math-academy-id "q-333710" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=z(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a10e8e34-584e-5dde-a768-87afe8799db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306662} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c30d1a-cecc-536a-acd4-56983a0e7313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306682} {:answer/id #uuid "c720718b-cfd5-517b-9025-a9fdd0097745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a10e8e34-584e-5dde-a768-87afe8799db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306662} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5c8e4a-3dcd-54af-923a-63f2223f1cac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b31cc06f-2c4a-5560-a82c-2939fb5a12a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306832} {:answer/id #uuid "d79730b9-e9f0-5545-95ff-2d98813ec20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307148} {:answer/id #uuid "ee849f27-95e1-5699-9b10-42370f974ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79730b9-e9f0-5545-95ff-2d98813ec20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307148} :answer-field/id #uuid "d265818d-b878-5a64-b86e-937f58280f38" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81336a83-b9fd-5624-b50d-8f56705ff853" :question/math-academy-id "q-333875" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2y-x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2y-x{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{18}=\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{25}{9}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{25}{9}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161d56e1-91dc-549e-9bfa-2ed40eaef4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305343} {:answer/id #uuid "5acf6f95-fec2-5eb9-953d-0d53c9f4569c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306019} {:answer/id #uuid "cecc9267-e1a8-57f9-bdbe-f13fc496322e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "161d56e1-91dc-549e-9bfa-2ed40eaef4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305343} :answer-field/id #uuid "372eed76-222e-597e-a32a-c4b7a330c7fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307698} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab8547f-f841-5304-954a-14363c4891cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305560} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7953f2-17f8-5608-8dad-19a64e0d71d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306015} {:answer/id #uuid "b635dc92-3533-5b4f-8832-27d4158c6c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b635dc92-3533-5b4f-8832-27d4158c6c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306853} :answer-field/id #uuid "da793013-b50c-5167-aa63-ac5232b9667e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b1c05ae-5800-59fe-b52e-f6754d3810af" :question/math-academy-id "q-333880" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =y-4{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-4{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{-1-4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}}=-9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2-\\frac{9}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0-4(-\\frac{5}{2})}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{40}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{40}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0-\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{20}{9}=-\\frac{5}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{40}{9}$ | $\\frac{20}{9}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{18}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{5}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈-\\frac{5}{18}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491bcea7-604e-509b-84a7-2a7ef2b97546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305862} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3d4145-687e-5558-90b1-dd695e53f467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306177} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea423d3-b31d-539a-a04d-0938105322e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3{(\\frac{x}{9})}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306366} {:answer/id #uuid "a52789ea-bc7a-5a0c-9009-d3ec390e0703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3{x}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306706} {:answer/id #uuid "e3105d73-1e15-5e75-9af6-10d80dda8afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "491bcea7-604e-509b-84a7-2a7ef2b97546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77e2814-9dc4-5042-a222-52ea6cb26c63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a61a3020-c49f-5adf-b3f7-a7d5dfccfd42" :question/math-academy-id "q-334402" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{4})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-4{p}^{3}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-4{p}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-4{p}^{3}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp-{p}^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{3})p-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =4{p}^{4}-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4{p}^{3} \\\\ y &=3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np={(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{({(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3})}^{4} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a84bd6a-a252-5e93-80a3-faf6510a6ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305396} {:answer/id #uuid "1f256702-41a7-51cc-957d-dcf8872e4779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305452} {:answer/id #uuid "31327022-0aa2-5f4e-b3e4-1263d3c1bac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305615} {:answer/id #uuid "c08aa8a5-296b-5bac-b092-299552f0c4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306946} {:answer/id #uuid "d467b8c9-b766-5228-a539-d0e56a389bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d467b8c9-b766-5228-a539-d0e56a389bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307122} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8cb3285-6e61-5807-836e-520c3dc4dff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d9b898f-a218-5ae2-bad8-8bb9a7b3c046" :question/math-academy-id "q-334453" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{{y}^{′}},\\,{y}^{′}\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\sqrt{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+\\sqrt{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}})p+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{p}{2\\sqrt{p}}+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}} \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{p}=-\\frac{1}{2x}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}},$ we must have $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $\\sqrt{p}$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2x})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149b9399-10a1-5499-82d6-47a004dd64e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305328} {:answer/id #uuid "44fae3d9-7df1-5bb3-a2b3-474d405440de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305828} {:answer/id #uuid "4da9d95f-808e-5bff-a256-146bbc6e66ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305905} {:answer/id #uuid "587c0f75-ac34-5edd-bb4e-8e6b92918419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306002} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9a1c62-1d7f-5c18-806d-ed540f16dfda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44fae3d9-7df1-5bb3-a2b3-474d405440de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305828} :answer-field/id #uuid "a799f118-0f61-529e-bf72-9b162ef1634a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2060631-04f7-58eb-86c9-92410b220532" :question/math-academy-id "q-334454" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+2({y}^{′})^{2}+3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+2({y}^{′})^{2}+3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+2{p}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+2{p}^{2}+3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+4p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+4p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+4p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-4p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+2{p}^{2}+3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =(-4p)p+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-4{p}^{2}+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4p \\\\ y &=-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$ \n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df4d9e4-5aab-5758-b6bf-f561a4a5bc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} {:answer/id #uuid "567f9cec-a4ac-5823-9ae9-ff55dac8a4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305989} {:answer/id #uuid "858bfa6f-4a26-5ea8-bf05-c82787e1d16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306424} {:answer/id #uuid "db9b03e9-0749-562a-b299-e3af23995ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{2}^{t},\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307187} {:answer/id #uuid "f30b8be4-1b5a-550e-a1eb-f3bf87eff617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df4d9e4-5aab-5758-b6bf-f561a4a5bc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} :answer-field/id #uuid "843487b0-a154-5051-ab18-86349bd94dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "360e7d39-e582-5724-9fad-4481a2f3b45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-33446" :question/problem "Find $(v(t)f(t))^{′}$ given that  $f(t)=⟨{2}^{t},\\,{2}^{t+1},\\,{2}^{2t}+1⟩$ and $v(t)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)f(t))^{′}=v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t+1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{2t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}})=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)f(t))^{′} & =v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t},\\,{2}^{t+1},\\,{2}^{2t}+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩-⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t+1}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154048e9-acbe-5f59-9e18-820fa18ba04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305335} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaff3a3-015e-5ac9-af35-ee4636f31af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i+2\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306017} {:answer/id #uuid "946e8ca8-3d7d-5f49-966f-fe22ae7ccf74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(-\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306552} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d8a392-1288-5247-80cb-5a23331deecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(2\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb277c8e-5a50-5fc4-b7fe-bc09685bb676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb277c8e-5a50-5fc4-b7fe-bc09685bb676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307478} :answer-field/id #uuid "9129fe60-9337-5fc0-b0ac-28b086b1002c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df456207-ebf6-517a-a452-6527239e70c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-33461" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{sin}^{2}t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j+{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d9ddb3-e9f1-5de0-bb06-57a20022d2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305187} {:answer/id #uuid "707c0e56-c5cf-52ea-843b-82853fc65446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306224} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c3c11c-b9e5-5fe5-8d56-aaba480c8e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "707c0e56-c5cf-52ea-843b-82853fc65446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306224} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdd5625-1f30-5f90-adab-e298d3f19999" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307827} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144753c1-56ee-5b3e-a2d7-fab92449ca64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305325} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbf4433-60a5-5d19-90bb-335c695857b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218630607