 #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305245} :answer-field/id #uuid "982eec34-4847-5cae-ab90-1bd0be9992a4" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308000} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046a5c1c-cbe9-5ac6-91f5-208820c1438a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305192} {:answer/id #uuid "04dcb767-dbd8-59a2-aa0c-318111e4e810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305196} {:answer/id #uuid "4172ea60-30de-50e3-9a6e-9486dbd007fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305796} {:answer/id #uuid "65c94625-57d0-57e4-84ab-725770edddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306129} {:answer/id #uuid "73b88285-e413-5a3b-93a4-9785c5c7edba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306251} {:answer/id #uuid "ec972956-22ec-53b6-b305-5b8cc4731f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04dcb767-dbd8-59a2-aa0c-318111e4e810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305196} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5af7f6c-4361-586e-b063-b2879ad83b84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c8db257-9425-559f-b6ed-3568cefb4a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305890} {:answer/id #uuid "69bc9b92-3f3c-5d90-9560-0fb382b1bde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306158} {:answer/id #uuid "7b34ea90-6bb0-5b0a-bec3-50f71b3ea8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306327} {:answer/id #uuid "926de338-d36c-57e2-b21d-f3fe0dda2327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306537} {:answer/id #uuid "acfc5f4f-52de-5af9-a7f4-fde42926aaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306784} {:answer/id #uuid "f7357c2d-369e-5705-a896-7ec65926269c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b34ea90-6bb0-5b0a-bec3-50f71b3ea8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306327} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0dbb9ac-bd25-551d-ba07-416287f50a99" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "529c6092-20b8-55fd-a14c-0946bb576ac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335071" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2{e}^{4x}+x,$ then $f=O({e}^{4x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,2{e}^{4x}=O(2{e}^{4x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{2{e}^{4x}})=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,x=O(2{e}^{4x})$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(4{e}^{4x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{4x})$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\n2{e}^{4x}=O(2{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand this follows by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{e}^{4x}=O(2{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx=O(2{e}^{4x}).\n$$\n\nSince $f=2{e}^{4x}+x,$ by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=2{e}^{4x}+x=O(2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{4x})=O(4{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nwhich justifies line $\\text{L4}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(4{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand by $\\text{the constant multiple property}$ with $c=4,$ we conclude\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{4x}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab888cde-11bb-5681-aa96-d5fbf0d0a5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab888cde-11bb-5681-aa96-d5fbf0d0a5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306769} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d4fcc78-3887-5c19-b9c0-ee1304276642" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65418582-7b22-5427-a2c2-990a6bb86b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65418582-7b22-5427-a2c2-990a6bb86b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306126} :answer-field/id #uuid "562ba54d-08de-5998-8e37-d1fae9a2d59d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45d5720b-f68d-5b35-9347-f940e6db3ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335115" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=xy+z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=x-y+2,\\, && z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=0\\cdot 1+0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=0-1+2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=\\frac{1}{2}-1+2=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4332b724-0400-5630-964e-201ec1ca6fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309256} {:answer/id #uuid "5143fe24-d030-572b-a92b-847e07a5e317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309363} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f0502-4184-5e94-8cd7-684cdf4cb1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310027} {:answer/id #uuid "db4f970d-9eef-504c-863f-23a1fee33248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310505} {:answer/id #uuid "e5203d5d-e4cc-53da-af11-7ac0b9f2d16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f0502-4184-5e94-8cd7-684cdf4cb1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310027} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0aa9e4b-44d3-5851-82d9-a6bf32d35ca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cef4bb70-8f2a-5e75-916b-a71e12c54640" :question/math-academy-id "q-33525" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}})⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}[-t⟨1,t⟩+(1+{t}^{2})⟨0,1⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-t,1⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩}{\\frac{1}{2}}=⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e2b089-092a-5eca-9f7e-bf626ffedb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} {:answer/id #uuid "71425229-5e0c-56e4-a3c5-6da6bfb56a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x/2})={e}^{x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309627} {:answer/id #uuid "cd378351-ace0-5392-8e0c-b064ad6a0d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2}))=-\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310393} {:answer/id #uuid "e71cd363-dab5-57e2-b1a7-f9cdab639247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}))=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310584} {:answer/id #uuid "f08a16be-ff29-5897-9836-28db90a5052f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e2b089-092a-5eca-9f7e-bf626ffedb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03a098b-971d-55f6-a30b-4a30fe70ddf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dba525fe-7e4d-5319-88bd-1b806feb0904" :question/math-academy-id "q-335278" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2}) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0802b9a4-f869-51aa-9ad2-f5c4ec4ede2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$19$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308807} {:answer/id #uuid "79af7521-db1a-5890-848a-9723cc01378d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} {:answer/id #uuid "851d5afb-e8fd-5832-aa19-7aad8ee508b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309784} {:answer/id #uuid "cc811dc3-bbd1-58f9-aae7-865a2b20e03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310387} {:answer/id #uuid "d7357cc4-db50-5841-9068-42178a678657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79af7521-db1a-5890-848a-9723cc01378d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} :answer-field/id #uuid "099de7c6-8163-5944-a855-904c946df6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ba6467f-d5e7-52b0-bc6e-1edae87450cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335280" :question/problem "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156ded23-ce88-53d3-a639-9d820eb09a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} {:answer/id #uuid "8a644610-216c-5c70-96be-6978f05c7dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309835} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4012d6-aa43-57f5-b933-67f5ae4bf20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309990} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fcc147-8682-560f-8b96-51d7e1858066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310305} {:answer/id #uuid "f721e0cb-70f5-51e1-acaf-e8fb8aacbddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156ded23-ce88-53d3-a639-9d820eb09a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f6fe49-3d76-5e25-8d26-c4ee0c65a685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "645d4a44-d927-5a86-b205-e23eb598edfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335282" :question/problem "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66392084-1375-5987-9665-329237969984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66392084-1375-5987-9665-329237969984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309534} :answer-field/id #uuid "34476173-6183-5e77-8805-215836d33152" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09961235-dce8-580e-a738-3b259302d637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09961235-dce8-580e-a738-3b259302d637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308817} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6e5623-fca0-5f8d-8044-35e6d67fcb0c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf9bfec2-a017-5355-86d4-1bd99de7e0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf9bfec2-a017-5355-86d4-1bd99de7e0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310292} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8581549-0f63-582c-a12a-710b4b47f75c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce30aec1-df7d-5ab3-aa4f-c5913ac5b56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335285" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=xz+1,\\, && y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=y+w-x,\\, && z\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=-yz,\\, && w\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(0),$ $z(0),$ and $w(0).$\n\n$\\,y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(0)≈$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xz+1 \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y+w-x \\\\  & =0+2-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-yz \\\\  & =-(0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{9}{4}$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈\\frac{1}{4},\\,z(0)≈\\frac{9}{4},\\,w(0)≈2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc3f0a39-79e1-58c3-8b1e-6eb28dab9173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc3f0a39-79e1-58c3-8b1e-6eb28dab9173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310762} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c3276ce-f686-5635-abb0-589f4354f103" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7146debe-9d15-5377-b8d5-31604ce997da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7146debe-9d15-5377-b8d5-31604ce997da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309628} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfdd59fb-2ae2-5cf5-b9e9-1012aae1ce49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "603e923e-e8b9-562b-95f3-0787d8481f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "603e923e-e8b9-562b-95f3-0787d8481f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475} :answer-field/id #uuid "cba15762-12df-5622-87b5-8227c24eb97f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a5a0630e-2c44-52ec-ba28-f49210f25d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335286" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2y-z+x,\\, && y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} &=z+w,\\, && z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\frac{xz}{2y},\\, && w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y-z+x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =z+w \\\\  & =1+(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =\\frac{xz}{2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 1}{2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{16}=\\frac{11}{16} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{3}{4})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=-2+\\frac{3}{16}=-\\frac{29}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{16}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{29}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{11}{16},\\,z(1)≈\\frac{1}{4},\\,w(1)≈-\\frac{29}{16}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d18eea3-d122-5337-ada3-b8e72f07364c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d18eea3-d122-5337-ada3-b8e72f07364c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309335} :answer-field/id #uuid "20449856-7f8b-5f6e-a299-b68ddc0254f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bf2c169-0160-5a24-894f-152cc48c06e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bf2c169-0160-5a24-894f-152cc48c06e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309081} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb9fda7-a464-5508-af9a-a92cdc30c73c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf4586e3-2707-56a0-afd6-6f3190313595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf4586e3-2707-56a0-afd6-6f3190313595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310414} :answer-field/id #uuid "bddd1180-37c6-5e7f-9932-44e0d4d4d075" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05bbc275-0c1d-5a37-b434-e108aeab7e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-335288" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=xy+z,\\, && y\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=x+w,\\, && z\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=3 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=-yz,\\, && w\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-1)+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+w \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-yz \\\\  & =-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  | $3$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  | $2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{7}{9}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{8}{9}$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{7}{9}=-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+\\frac{8}{9}=\\frac{35}{9} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{7}{9}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{8}{9}$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{2}{9}$ |  |  | $\\frac{35}{9}$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈-\\frac{2}{9},\\,z(1)≈\\frac{35}{9},\\,w(1)≈3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09da0c17-a8dd-53af-8502-57a2f6d19294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09da0c17-a8dd-53af-8502-57a2f6d19294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308820} :answer-field/id #uuid "166c277a-23e3-5352-b0c1-bda4b2bd945d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fada4b06-f79c-5f64-8ca8-ad93c3812fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fada4b06-f79c-5f64-8ca8-ad93c3812fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310746} :answer-field/id #uuid "4572000c-c2bb-5fe3-85e6-d2f8c06028bf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35fde8ba-b3e9-50f7-a313-3c2425f36c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35fde8ba-b3e9-50f7-a313-3c2425f36c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309158} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7669251-3415-5c44-8e7b-ff5aff0d9040" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80e26b48-6b80-5a39-a769-da709c07b88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335290" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=y+z+w,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=xy-{w}^{2},\\, && z(0)=-2 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\frac{yz}{w},\\, && w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ $z\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and $w\\,(\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y+z+w \\\\  & =1+(-2)+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xy-{w}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =\\frac{yz}{w} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $-2$ | $-1$ |  | $1$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1,\\,z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈-\\frac{5}{2},\\,w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e7144f4-594b-52a0-aff1-94cda2bef455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e7144f4-594b-52a0-aff1-94cda2bef455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309600} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a44e463-cacf-525d-b2f1-44d53aa53e6c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a06a4bd-2685-5292-8e5f-d1df5cb41dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a06a4bd-2685-5292-8e5f-d1df5cb41dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309062} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1f0975-6112-59