 :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda26093-935a-5428-8a4a-313dc7eef261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310638} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7cb1954-825c-587d-9ae0-71b65bb89546" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dfc78a4-9775-5274-a0d6-f79a2fab4840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dfc78a4-9775-5274-a0d6-f79a2fab4840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310023} :answer-field/id #uuid "b01c8457-5c61-5041-b912-5831ab12ed1a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "864b79e9-e88b-5d7e-ad0a-199bd64476ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-335319" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2-x+z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=3y-2z+w,\\, && z(0)=-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=1-y,\\, && w(0)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=-1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2-x+z \\\\  & =2-0+(-1) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3y-2z+w \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)+0 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =1-y \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $-1$ | $5$ |  | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+1=0 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{6}{5}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈\\frac{6}{5},$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈0,$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18747b7f-5c38-50e4-b76b-cbdacfbbbdcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18747b7f-5c38-50e4-b76b-cbdacfbbbdcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308942} :answer-field/id #uuid "80349b1c-8998-5a85-8c88-2d9e46b4f78d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de8432f4-9e54-531a-ad60-95a4b5764083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8432f4-9e54-531a-ad60-95a4b5764083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310534} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8b8eee-9a9f-58d3-baf7-83950f61f7ad" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a6586b1-4319-5ed3-a1e1-7b2ce9e52381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a6586b1-4319-5ed3-a1e1-7b2ce9e52381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309993} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafd03b4-9f50-5ce0-871f-45375d15cebc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9296fae-1fa2-567a-b401-9d7d4a676496" :question/math-academy-id "q-335365" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &={x}^{2}+3y-z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{x+1}{w}-y,\\, && z(0)=0 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\sqrt[3]{y+z+7},\\, && w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$, $z$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y-z \\\\  & ={0}^{2}+3\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x+1}{w}-y \\\\  & =\\frac{0+1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {w}^{′} & =\\sqrt[3]{y+z+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+0+7} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |  | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+3=4 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $4$ |  |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈4,$\n$z(1)≈-\\frac{1}{2},$\nand\n$w(1)≈4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d23eb7-e15b-5305-87e4-a946eeb83ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d23eb7-e15b-5305-87e4-a946eeb83ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309359} :answer-field/id #uuid "65264dfd-d790-5773-966a-e2af1ddf877c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a40ab8d7-d0b2-5cf8-b5f7-4b99b31f53fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335456" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(1<X<4).$\n\n$P(1<X<4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<4) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4})+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da8c0f3-1f7c-5e08-bb0c-0291b66ff2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3310" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da8c0f3-1f7c-5e08-bb0c-0291b66ff2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3310" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308840} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dfb198f-924a-5e7f-b0ae-4192a5533170" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdab4603-a016-5154-99e4-069b9603f000" :question/math-academy-id "q-335457" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{8}),$ compute $P(2<X<7)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(2<X<7)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{8}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{8}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<7) & =P(X\\in {3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{8})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{8})+{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{8})+{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{4}(\\frac{3}{8})+{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{5}(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & ≈0.3310.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51737088-92ab-5b29-82ac-e537449f6038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309365} {:answer/id #uuid "526f6ddb-2bfd-5535-a784-04786b110605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309371} {:answer/id #uuid "942a8856-2dbb-5570-a5b2-9a4b4bd533d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309935} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaf600f-9098-5544-a753-cf5c4c16901e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310160} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cd2225-859d-5768-a4c5-fd53001d89c3" :answer