03-5a3f-9e6d-713b90d2c7f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-335474" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5,$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{5})=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O(\\underbrace{4{x}^{2}-x+5}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $5$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\text{the constant multiple property}$ that\n\n$$\nf=O(1)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064fbd9c-80ed-51b0-98cb-32bd23adfd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308800} {:answer/id #uuid "131e40fb-788f-5311-aa90-2ba7a9c2a470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308884} {:answer/id #uuid "604d06b0-499e-5ae6-b383-8e4f9aff6004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309476} {:answer/id #uuid "7489e527-2be8-5430-b0a7-0c197eae2f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309652} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed2b579-2a1c-5028-941b-30fd3eb330b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed2b579-2a1c-5028-941b-30fd3eb330b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309738} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b15e461-76ed-5fe3-95c8-709b91e6e409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "169c8d2a-baf7-5d16-8418-7c154f48a72f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335578" :question/problem "Throughout an experiment in a laboratory, a culture contains $1\\,200$ bacteria in total (limited by nutrients and space). At the beginning of the experiment, $200$ of the $1\\,200$ bacteria are resistant to a new antibiotic. After $4$ hours, the number of resistant bacteria increases to $500.$ To the nearest hour, how long does it take for the number of resistant bacteria to reach $900?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of resistant bacteria, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in hours.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the resistant bacteria population, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total bacteria population in the culture and its current resistant population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total bacteria population the culture can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,200$ bacteria, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,200-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,200-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,200-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,200-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial population of resistant bacteria was $200$ cells. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=200,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K{e}^{0} & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K & =200 \\\\ K & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $4$ hours, the resistant bacteria population was $500$ cells. So, applying the condition $P(4)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-4k} & =500 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,000{e}^{-4k} \\\\ {e}^{-4k} & =\\frac{700}{1\\,000}=\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $900$ bacteria are resistant to the antibiotic. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =900 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =900 \\\\ 300 & =1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{300}{1\\,000}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $900$ bacteria will be resistant to the antibiotic after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})}{-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})}=13.502…≈14\\,\\text{hours},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bab6fed-c38d-5347-ae71-c7210567374e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308829} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6b6102-1988-5f44-921b-dab11741888b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309101} {:answer/id #uuid "3171d56f-a010-58c5-9741-0926362161c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309122} {:answer/id #uuid "33c66e55-e3c5-5aa9-a78e-57027777e26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309143} {:answer/id #uuid "82fe0cee-6b18-5b50-ba64-a1713c165400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82fe0cee-6b18-5b50-ba64-a1713c165400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6736f81-1f81-5c62-8e0a-cc99c0f20ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b1aa988-c2c7-5b7c-8814-04c3301ea2f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-33581" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e70df3-e69a-52ce-adaf-9d80576fc124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309306} {:answer/id #uuid "4de60ca3-08eb-5908-84e7-337d050635cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309339} {:answer/id #uuid "60127d29-07b2-5438-b25c-538c97234290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309472} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e91b0c-4363-5464-927a-584f15d25ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310236} {:answer/id #uuid "e373d4bf-5114-5201-a0ca-5837cf70ed2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7e91b0c-4363-5464-927a-584f15d25ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310236} :answer-field/id #uuid "964f9aee-69e3-5eaf-ba62-46327eb55e3f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fd4c10d-4104-533e-a7ea-701ca41dc419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309354} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab118d1-7dd3-5f85-b172-3299a04f369d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309705} {:answer/id #uuid "976ecd48-d13c-560a-802a-8e7cf948da05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309962} {:answer/id #uuid "de8dc48e-6d1c-5472-ac5b-8b94b9419130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "976ecd48-d13c-560a-802a-8e7cf948da05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309962} :answer-field/id #uuid "fba4b49a-a123-5ea4-a04f-61aaf7f9c4c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bbe03fd-0d2c-564c-9b93-a7997b3f7696" :question/math-academy-id "q-335812" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},\\,x>0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yx & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2C}{\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311638 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea01709-12b4-58a6-ab2e-4962013826ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea01709-12b4-58a6-ab2e-4962013826ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309345} :answer-field/id #uuid "31904c2b-2a8f-532b-964d-ea9d4fd7f84e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a594be-b7bf-590a-ba97-77efa196cdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40a594be-b7bf-590a-ba97-77efa196cdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309240} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dce419-ace0-5455-9896-3d1050329377" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21ef4a3a-3143-5d28-9b7b-5c36663b99e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-335917" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}ln\\,({t}^{2}),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}ln\\,({t}^{2})=2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(2t)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,({e}^{-3/2}) & ={e}^{-3/2}(4ln\\,({e}^{-3/2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(4\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(-6+2) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t={e}^{-3/2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4{e}^{-3/2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0c2b4c-3635-5650-a4d5-2b4fcb2458c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e0c2b4c-3635-5650-a4d5-2b4fcb2458c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309879} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f602e95-584a-5023-86c3-436dd72e8776" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311197} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1c50573-2cfe-55fe-9e22-9104f673ef53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c50573-2cfe-55fe-9e22-9104f673ef53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310674} :answer-field/id #uuid "db687731-0abe-581c-9757-eabd90c503db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb520858-1659-5942-b7f5-935661020acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335924" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-1 \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "606ae851-a7d5-58a3-8e79-a9296cf345d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}t+t\\operatorname{ln}t{csc}^{2}t}{t{cot}^{2}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606ae851-a7d5-58a3-8e79-a9296cf345d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}t+t\\operatorname{ln}t{csc}^{2}t}{t{cot}^{2}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309478} :answer-field/id #uuid "d339cd93-b3fc-51f1-96cc-794e009be99e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "123379b4-b283-5d57-933b-9e195e37586e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336130" :question/problem "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t(-{csc}^{2}⁡t))}{{cot}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{csc}^{2}⁡t}{t})}{{cot}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{csc}^{2}⁡t}{t{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2e839e-2d16-5ab9-82e9-ba60af1493b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e2e839e-2d16-5ab9-82e9-ba60af1493b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309096} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a614ab-94ad-50cc-9f84-fa1b40773b66" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f0cc14-d5b7-54ca-a41d-0d31e9ada394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51f0cc14-d5b7-54ca-a41d-0d31e9ada394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309369} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c80b44-75e6-5aa0-b7b5-b96d76d680e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e51aabe-7c8b-5e7f-b26a-fff33d15da87" :question/math-academy-id "q-336166" :question/problem "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)=\\frac{{2}^{t}}{{2}^{t}+1}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the position ${s}_{0}$ and velocity ${v}_{0}$ of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n${s}_{0}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}\\,\\text{and}\\,$\n\n${v}_{0}=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)=\\frac{{2}^{t}}{{2}^{t}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}}{{2}^{0}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{2}^{t}}{{2}^{t}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({2}^{t}+1)\\cdot {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-{2}^{t}\\cdot {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{t}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{t}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{0}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "630b07f3-19d9-587b-98cc-6eadf089910b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "630b07f3-19d9-587b-98cc-6eadf089910b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309502} :answer-field/id #uuid "2adcb2ad-a9b6-5339-b240-1c3840f7f10b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62c296d3-db75-5f2c-8e72-9a9c1243244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336174" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in feet) at time $t$ minutes is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=1\\,\\text{min}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ft/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=1,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-4|=4\\,\\text{ft/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84cf384-665b-50e1-bf2a-ca075c794e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c84cf384-665b-50e1-bf2a-ca075c794e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310356} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff8a143f-e554-5de9-b25f-8be9ed5f5803" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9a84fb2-0a5b-573b-b24a-f2106d53fb89" :question/math-academy-id "q-336192" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your answer to 2 decimal places.\n\nThe speed of the particle is approximately\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}|=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}≈2.47\\,\\text{m/s}$ to 2 decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f153927-df76-5146-8ac8-5288980adcaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f153927-df76-5146-8ac8-5288980adcaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309348} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef25fd6-8083-59ef-8e8f-d3e3ec185739" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310916} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "969c6421-29b1-5864-9f20-e9bbe9ff643b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969c6421-29b1-5864-9f20-e9bbe9ff643b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309956} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db330ac-7f57-597d-8f8b-bfe23d2af45d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16161174-d576-5a75-8e50-4d2a191b2311" :question/math-academy-id "q-336322" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0,$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{3})=-0.339\\,836…≈-0.3398\n$$\n\nSince $-0.3398$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.3398.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.3398.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.3398.$\n\n![](images/ed/ed9b300e3f4f79298feba6a2d61347d938ce64d7100ad7b0696e13dbe2a65238.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.3398 \\\\  & ≈3.4814\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & ≈3.4814-2\\pi ≈-2.8018\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈-2.8,\\,x≈-0.3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04802603-7504-5546-a3be-3c58893aa391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04802603-7504-5546-a3be-3c58893aa391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308779} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8da0e59-e758-50ad-b223-814077cc3178" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16287804-f34d-52ea-a18f-36c3f78a5bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-336415" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{3{t}^{2}-5t+2}{t}.$ Calculate the acceleration $a$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3{t}^{2}-5t+2}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-5+\\frac{2}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-5+2{t}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3+2(-{t}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a84c30-999e-5b85-990b-140839e9bac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a84c30-999e-5b85-990b-140839e9bac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310192} :answer-field/id #uuid "a569186b-6556-5dc3-a0cf-ed483c62ff4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe99fc7f-3d36-51f3-b292-37172f0cbe8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336444" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+4\\sqrt{t},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=1$ second.\n\n$a(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4{t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2t+2{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =2+2(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1$ second, we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1)^{3}}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a94b79a9-1d72-50ed-b307-e0236ebfd2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94b79a9-1d72-50ed-b307-e0236ebfd2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310116} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd462a0b-256b-50ea-b79b-4b0d8704919b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac45128c-c263-5b74-919e-49d0aa7b4d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-336449" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}+3t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ second.\n\n$a(\\frac{1}{2})=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}+3t) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}-2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-2t+3) \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ second, we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{1}{2}) & =24{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f63e4b9-9298-5ae3-b435-8926ee6d39df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f63e4b9-9298-5ae3-b435-8926ee6d39df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310035} :answer-field/id #uuid "d80766e7-8eda-58d3-95cb-0c873ef5d68f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18ee446d-9856-56cd-b0ee-9f267f88a8bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-336633" :question/problem "You have a stack of seven cards labeled $1$ through $7.$ If one card is randomly drawn, what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the label on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S={1,2,3,…,7},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{7}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(2)=…=f(7)=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+…+7\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+…+7(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{2}{7}+…+\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4+5+6+7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{7} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10332c7c-88ec-558c-9e68-cd596e409227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10332c7c-88ec-558c-9e68-cd596e409227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308863} :answer-field/id #uuid "af4f7463-b19f-56ae-8fd9-1a020880060e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "344bd66a-fb8d-5b1c-95de-b6559c935b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336640" :question/problem "What is the expected value of one roll of a fair tetrahedral die with sides labeled $1,5,7,8,$ respectively?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the roll of a four-sided die. Then the support is $S={1,5,7,8},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{4}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(5)=f(7)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+5\\cdot f(5)+7\\cdot f(7)+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+5(\\frac{1}{4})+7(\\frac{1}{4})+8(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+5+7+8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4} \\\\  & =5.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5821c521-de90-55f6-aa03-7929089c6fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5821c521-de90-55f6-aa03-7929089c6fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309414} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4c622f1-5761-5cba-a0e7-2ebff10b66ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3039f274-1c53-50a8-a836-70d3154fa917" :question/math-academy-id "q-336697" :question/problem "The first digit of a serial number can be $2$ through $8$ with equal likelihood. If a part for a machine is selected from a large batch, what is the expected value for the first digit of the part's serial number?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the first digit of the serial number of the part. Then the support is $S={2,3,4,5,6,7,8},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{7}:$\n\n$$\nf(2)=f(3)=…=f(8)=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+…+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{7})+3(\\frac{1}{7})+…+8(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{3}{7}+…+\\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+3+4+5+6+7+8}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{7} \\\\  & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "752af0da-dbae-5520-b62b-cf96e0283d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{11}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "752af0da-dbae-5520-b62b-cf96e0283d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{11}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309661} :answer-field/id #uuid "09205559-5a9a-53bf-8f0c-47fbdcd2d6a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "999290d3-e880-5065-9964-ee6f1b8bd7a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336717" :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4x-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 3 \\\\ u & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{(4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{3}^{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}ln\\,(\\frac{11}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346c66fa-7cd0-5539-a484-a3d7c3d37a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "346c66fa-7cd0-5539-a484-a3d7c3d37a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309147} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f925dd-1cec-502d-a118-9bf74b6846b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb5be3ea-fe7c-5ca2-a3b9-f4f0c7f3fd0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336729" :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{5{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5{x}^{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =20{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =20{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5{x}^{4}+4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{(5{x}^{4}+4)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a263b98-3c02-57c9-8756-64a094c38144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a263b98-3c02-57c9-8756-64a094c38144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309424} :answer-field/id #uuid "753e0896-4b34-5f55-acf5-5d588634bdcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf7adf0d-4bcf-5060-b9cf-487729e3f0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-336751" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b304da0cca6e678fd53931c6949ff51546486df844553e79608542a8bf7886d.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=10I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{29},$ $\\beta =\\frac{15}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{29})}^{2}+{(\\frac{15}{29})}^{2}}≈0.56\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dca3b97-04f1-5f9f-a8b9-0899d217acdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dca3b97-04f1-5f9f-a8b9-0899d217acdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310021} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e77ff9-eb58-54f5-8ca8-af2a430b9253" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59a6d12d-9329-5b47-9600-07de9441f681" :question/math-academy-id "q-336758" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{3{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2086e10e-bf6b-570b-9e79-ede9a646fe9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308994} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb11ed9-985f-544c-8920-3388c7a6519f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309444} {:answer/id #uuid "9168ec64-3a53-5b16-9c62-1d367dbacf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309912} {:answer/id #uuid "aa550866-db3a-5d4c-8873-53cc1c2abea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310125} {:answer/id #uuid "fc13a552-61c6-552f-9a18-b4b5b329247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa550866-db3a-5d4c-8873-53cc1c2abea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310125} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac3a2c81-3ab1-5fd1-97e5-0673d9615ba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "730871e0-d608-50a9-8e24-089b6fc0a471" :question/math-academy-id "q-33676" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve   $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,2{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩$  at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b83fcff9-b155-591f-95ee-0c33ecb6e183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b83fcff9-b155-591f-95ee-0c33ecb6e183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310238} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a54632-3d44-5eb8-80f1-61e506b9cc79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43329e32-a27f-5472-a41e-d6bebac9271e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336777" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-4}}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5789afad-b84f-5f8d-b48e-643fcc282e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5789afad-b84f-5f8d-b48e-643fcc282e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309405} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a9cf4b1-bba2-5b45-abc8-21932ce7b3bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b11af32-1fc9-5539-bcdc-5bfdb12c2b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336782" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{-2{csc}^{2}⁡(2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2{csc}^{2}⁡(2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}{2(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}^{2}}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot 4{sin}^{2}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{4{cos}^{3}⁡(x)\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}}{2\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}2{cos}^{3}⁡(x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{3}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e57333-adfd-5f69-ab27-c8e5ee35309b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6e57333-adfd-5f69-ab27-c8e5ee35309b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310480} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afeaa15-4597-5565-9d95-3bd8ba39fdee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73bc5fc3-eca8-5d40-a62e-f3da22b5f1ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-336787" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}]^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}}{\\cancel{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a1f7051-76a8-5e30-aadf-4a572c67a20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a1f7051-76a8-5e30-aadf-4a572c67a20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309569} :answer-field/id #uuid "da832720-b643-5978-9ddd-fd0ef7ee4a2b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4980fc43-3957-5137-8a22-f74496d06d0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336798" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are  ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and  ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7abab752-1b7c-5b52-9b1c-6ac3d6705441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7abab752-1b7c-5b52-9b1c-6ac3d6705441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309707} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d2a1527-abc0-5c97-aa16-af52abb1ecd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90c07f78-de76-5544-8060-f4dd40c8aa0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336802" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{[ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)]}^{′}}{{[ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x})}{(-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}{\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be47698d-6716-5543-a576-b889b9ad0123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be47698d-6716-5543-a576-b889b9ad0123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310286} :answer-field/id #uuid "633bd358-5572-5dfc-9db8-1db23f5403cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12541a95-3ed9-5a78-aeb0-f1bac7373e1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336818" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a2cc9b62f3eeed4f2c9559c7f8cc5646872bc43b8076bea92bae50c3c6b29a9.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +8\\beta &=12\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{6}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}}≈1.34\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a72a30e-8e1f-5cff-a823-b28d24dfd363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a72a30e-8e1f-5cff-a823-b28d24dfd363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309575} :answer-field/id #uuid "c055905a-e4d7-5477-85f8-afe4f4ba006a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50835fd0-094c-5563-a823-9e7a69e0be6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336948" :question/problem "If $f(x)=9{x}^{6}$ and $g(x)=-4{x}^{6},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=$  \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{(-4{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fada8a6-7710-59de-b52e-f184d45cd037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fada8a6-7710-59de-b52e-f184d45cd037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309896} :answer-field/id #uuid "396a4919-1957-541b-9cd1-791e4c556a1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e34adca9-52ea-5918-9113-f6b8e7bf7260" :question/math-academy-id "q-336971" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67dde790-7f11-5809-b438-3a8f72e27b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67dde790-7f11-5809-b438-3a8f72e27b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309547} :answer-field/id #uuid "04982ddd-0975-5db7-9d2d-dd2d9b510a36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9ed81b2-da4e-505d-aa76-858248f5472c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337183" :question/problem "The total number of heads after $4$ flips of an unfair coin follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the total number of heads after $4$ flips of the coin? Do not round the answer.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{21}$ | $\\frac{1}{21}$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{3}{7})+1(\\frac{2}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+3(\\frac{2}{21})+4(\\frac{1}{21}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+6+6+4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "745cd620-d58c-5213-8b22-00c45051d187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "745cd620-d58c-5213-8b22-00c45051d187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309651} :answer-field/id #uuid "47a334c4-6c7c-5268-ae87-927d688eb150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c04aba30-34c3-525b-a65a-fd4f50fe4a8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337195" :question/problem "A technician reviews inspection logs for defective components. The number of defects found per inspection is summarized below. If $X$ is the number of defective components found in an inspection, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Inspections | $5$ | $7$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Defects | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the defects, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of inspections is $5+7+4+3+1=20,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{7}{20} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ P(X=4)=f(4) & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ | $\\frac{4}{20}$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{5}{20})+1(\\frac{7}{20})+2(\\frac{4}{20})+3(\\frac{3}{20})+4(\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{8}{20}+\\frac{9}{20}+\\frac{4}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54cd75b7-ccd0-57b5-863b-eb7c58a8576d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54cd75b7-ccd0-57b5-863b-eb7c58a8576d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309390} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb36d74-6d2b-5ab4-b3a3-5917a15c36fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dea2be0e-fdc0-5db0-86e3-22ebe1a7a0ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-337197" :question/problem "A production analyst records the number of flawed units in sampled batches. The data are summarized below. If $X$ is the number of flawed units in a batch, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Batches | $10$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Flawed Units | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the flawed units, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of batches is $10+9+6=25,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{25} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{9}{25} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{10}{25}$ | $\\frac{9}{25}$ | $\\frac{6}{25}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{10}{25})+1(\\frac{9}{25})+2(\\frac{6}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fbce183-67c5-562d-9e07-ecff5e52dd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fbce183-67c5-562d-9e07-ecff5e52dd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309746} :answer-field/id #uuid "37dad391-7d9a-56ae-9376-8a79bdb3732a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37a09e85-5b87-5925-be0a-eeab91b0e860" :question/math-academy-id "q-337198" :question/problem "A quality assurance specialist inspects sealed containers for damaged items. The number of damaged items in each container was recorded and summarized below. If $X$ is the number of damaged items in a container, what is the expected value of $X?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| Number of Containers | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Damaged Items | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the damaged items, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of containers is $10+8+6+4+2=30,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{30} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{8}{30} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{6}{30} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{4}{30} \\\\ P(X=4)=f(4) & =\\frac{2}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{10}{30}$ | $\\frac{8}{30}$ | $\\frac{6}{30}$ | $\\frac{4}{30}$ | $\\frac{2}{30}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{10}{30})+1(\\frac{8}{30})+2(\\frac{6}{30})+3(\\frac{4}{30})+4(\\frac{2}{30}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{30}+\\frac{12}{30}+\\frac{12}{30}+\\frac{8}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4ff13e-f340-5dac-83ed-8fb52d7b02fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e4ff13e-f340-5dac-83ed-8fb52d7b02fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309219} :answer-field/id #uuid "37e031e8-21b3-530b-a635-219edc001543" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d888cbf-5ba1-5939-80dc-812c7eab30b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337202" :question/problem "A stack of cards has $6$ cards labeled with two, $6$ cards labeled with five, and $8$ cards labeled with eleven. If one card is randomly drawn from the stack, what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S={2,5,11}.$\n\nIn the stack there are $6$ cards labeled with two, $6$ cards labeled with five, and $8$ cards labeled with eleven (twenty cards in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{6}{20}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(5) & =\\frac{6}{20}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(11) & =\\frac{8}{20}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+5\\cdot f(5)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{10})+5(\\frac{3}{10})+11(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{22}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{44}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+15+44}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc294a0a-6e2b-5607-aa58-b7b3cec45ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc294a0a-6e2b-5607-aa58-b7b3cec45ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310384} :answer-field/id #uuid "95680416-9d87-507b-ac1c-73b662b31346" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8fea60d-8acd-505a-bf62-74afec768282" :question/math-academy-id "q-337206" :question/problem "A spinner has $1$ section labeled with two, $2$ sections labeled with four, $3$ sections labeled with six, and $4$ sections labeled with eight. If the spinner is spun once,  what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value shown on a randomly spun spinner. Then the support is $S={2,4,6,8}.$\n\nOn the spinner there are $1$ section labeled with two, $2$ sections labeled with four, $3$ sections labeled with six, and $4$ sections labeled with eight (ten sections in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(4) & =\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(6) & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(8) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+4\\cdot f(4)+6\\cdot f(6)+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{10})+4(\\frac{1}{5})+6(\\frac{3}{10})+8(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{8}{10}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+18+32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{10} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ff55b31-22fd-5f70-8686-2adbcd29fc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff55b31-22fd-5f70-8686-2adbcd29fc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309750} :answer-field/id #uuid "83917d28-9206-599b-a469-91d29dde2a87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd95e477-b127-56b9-8e98-54410e77db77" :question/math-academy-id "q-337207" :question/problem "A bag has $4$ tiles labeled with two, $5$ tiles labeled with seven, and $3$ tiles labeled with fifteen. If one tile is randomly drawn from the bag, what is its expected value? Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn tile. Then the support is $S={2,7,15}.$\n\nIn the bag, there are $5$ tiles labeled with seven, $4$ tiles labeled with two, and $3$ tiles labeled with fifteen (twelve tiles in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(7) & =\\frac{5}{12} \\\\ P(15) & =\\frac{3}{12}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+7\\cdot f(7)+15\\cdot f(15) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3})+7(\\frac{5}{12})+15(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{35}{12}+\\frac{15}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12}+\\frac{35}{12}+\\frac{45}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{8+35+45}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{88}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4066c9ef-2808-556d-82ae-df01f268a997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4066c9ef-2808-556d-82ae-df01f268a997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309238} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce0b1fd-e8e2-56d1-a409-aed2c888b0ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69a23de5-cd06-5d46-a486-07e0ef32be2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337208" :question/problem "A jar has $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve. If one chip is randomly drawn from the jar,  what is its expected value? Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn chip. Then the support is $S={1,4,9,12}.$\n\nIn the jar, there are $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve (fifteen chips in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =\\frac{6}{15}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ P(4) & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ P(9) & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(12) & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+4\\cdot f(4)+9\\cdot f(9)+12\\cdot f(12) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{5})+4(\\frac{4}{15})+9(\\frac{1}{5})+12(\\frac{2}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{27}{15}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+16+27+24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f30852-47f0-5a6b-87db-ac15a818f355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309003} {:answer/id #uuid "be83f521-1513-5563-aaca-88655bd63836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be83f521-1513-5563-aaca-88655bd63836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310289} :answer-field/id #uuid "012b9d81-72aa-5987-ac92-34b0607afc69" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440c7798-a7c8-5e8d-af50-ab328a69acba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309266} {:answer/id #uuid "8057bf1d-3e44-5e64-abc2-f9b73cc06815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309755} {:answer/id #uuid "dfde9415-e6b6-59d5-bd4d-05c090ef9fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310543} {:answer/id #uuid "eb567668-eecc-548b-a97e-c8268574a4f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310620} {:answer/id #uuid "ee890229-7196-5863-ae88-890c5a09d2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310650}] :answer-field/