r/value "-y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309452} {:answer/id #uuid "7d272730-8c43-5f09-8db0-59b10b8f73da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309726} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ae8858-301e-5226-90a6-67ed8f08bf23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310561} {:answer/id #uuid "f67a1723-ca4e-57e1-b221-16f748121553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2ae8858-301e-5226-90a6-67ed8f08bf23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310561} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d5015f-28f9-57aa-90c8-d719ecf2fb7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62b86b0d-e565-5d2b-9468-fd43079be716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309496} {:answer/id #uuid "8723bd22-b99e-5e57-af22-bfa26d52d0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62b86b0d-e565-5d2b-9468-fd43079be716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309496} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0bf2f1f-20ef-51d1-aeea-0db06c5d17f7" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311261} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8636a482-da7c-53f4-b210-d3375a6a85f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309798} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a58c5c-e532-561e-b9bd-b42bfda6b620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8636a482-da7c-53f4-b210-d3375a6a85f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309798} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb48e22-5055-593a-8692-5c588dc1a00c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e2b872a-4e35-5059-b33b-25da543bae2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337259" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}({x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})=\\frac{2x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{isn't}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ $\\text{isn't}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "631650c5-379d-5261-8d8a-7fc93c8eead7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631650c5-379d-5261-8d8a-7fc93c8eead7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504} :answer-field/id #uuid "57532e6a-7bb5-5f10-9513-13628527c8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "026c9bf9-47c1-55e7-813b-b60eba94c35d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337334" :question/problem "Consider the following game featuring an unfair six-sided die. The probability that the die shows an odd number is $\\frac{2}{3},$ and the probability that the die shows an even number is $\\frac{1}{3}.$ If the roll of a die gives an odd number, the player loses $$4.$ If the roll of a die gives an even number, the player wins $$11.$ What is the expected profit of each roll in this game?\n\n$\\text{E}[p]=$${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$, $4,$ or $6$), the player wins $$11,$ so we have $p=11.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-4)=\\frac{2}{3},\\,P(11)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-4$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-4)\\cdot f(-4)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =(-4)(\\frac{2}{3})+11(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$1.00.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b22f6c61-7f29-5cf9-9abf-e349bcdd6754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22f6c61-7f29-5cf9-9abf-e349bcdd6754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310187} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bffc3e3-bb56-5445-ac62-ee87caec2e84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01f6d81f-9b7a-55ac-b9f3-3625291ed8bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-337335" :question/problem "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2.$ The outcomes of the coin have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{1}{4},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8,$ so we have $p=8.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $8$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =8\\cdot f(8)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+(-2)(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4}-\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$0.50.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "279ce0dd-a0b3-5eb7-a2ad-695e43c2266c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "279ce0dd-a0b3-5eb7-a2ad-695e43c2266c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309047} :answer-field/id #uuid "97607d3d-11e1-5b39-8972-09c5b7d53481" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d5c7f49-65ed-5617-8670-c9074e9f79de" :question/math-academy-id "q-337336" :question/problem "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4.$ The coin outcomes have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{7}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $5$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =5\\cdot f(5)+(-4)\\cdot f(-4) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3}{10})+(-4)(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{28}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-28}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =-1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.30.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b73fb6d-8a9e-5a93-8b92-7f1113c9aca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b73fb6d-8a9e-5a93-8b92-7f1113c9aca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309712} :answer-field/id #uuid "51729311-af8f-561b-9b59-ec2449d82979" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa822380-c2c7-527f-9d92-45c44d685010" :question/math-academy-id "q-337337" :question/problem "Suppose we play a raffle that costs $$2$ per day. Each player chooses a four-digit number between $0000$ and $9999,$ inclusive, and one winning number is randomly selected each day. The owner of the chosen number each day wins $$2,000.$ What is the expected daily profit of a player in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the daily profit in this game. There are $10,000$ possible numbers that can be selected. One of these numbers is the winning number. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the player's number is selected, they win $$2,000.$ This means the player’s profit is $$2,000-$2=$1,998,$ so we have $p=1998.$\n\n- If the player's number is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(1998)=\\frac{1}{10,000},\\,P(-2)=1-\\frac{1}{10,000}=\\frac{9,999}{10,000}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $1998$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{10,000}$ | $\\frac{9,999}{10,000}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1998\\cdot f(1998)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =1998(\\frac{1}{10,000})-2(\\frac{9,999}{10,000}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1998}{10,000}-\\frac{19,998}{10,000} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18,000}{10,000} \\\\  & =-1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538ed980-c655-591a-832d-b79489a64509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "538ed980-c655-591a-832d-b79489a64509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309382} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d251926-590f-58d5-b970-6b66e539eca0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d50f52c5-de38-5f6e-81ff-83b569c76b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-337338" :question/problem "Suppose we play a game that costs $$2$ per round. Each player randomly selects one card from a standard $52$-card deck. If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. What is the expected profit of a player in this game?\n\nRounded to two decimal places, the expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit in this game. There are $52$ possible cards that can be selected. One of these cards is the winning card. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ This means the player’s profit is $$100-$2=$98,$ so we have $p=98.$\n\n- If the ace of spades is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(98)=\\frac{1}{52},\\,P(-2)=\\frac{51}{52}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $98$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{52}$ | $\\frac{51}{52}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =98\\cdot f(98)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =98(\\frac{1}{52})-2(\\frac{51}{52}) \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{52}-\\frac{102}{52} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is approximately $-$\\,0.08.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b72fc64-c373-5e5e-9153-9c50fe35bacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b72fc64-c373-5e5e-9153-9c50fe35bacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309846} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b920ed6-1baf-5d39-a633-04132831f34b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fb4ad17-dc0d-5ff1-a890-1558591b2dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-337341" :question/problem "Consider a game of chance with $4$ prizes ($$1$, $$2$, $$3$, and $$5$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$1$ | $25%$ |\n| $$2$ | $25%$ |\n| $$3$ | $30%$ |\n| $$5$ | $20%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game, in dollars. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table. If $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{3}{10})+5(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5+10+18+20}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{20} \\\\  & =2.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$\\,2.65.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6ec8c42-42eb-5be2-92ea-5c55a2bb083e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ec8c42-42eb-5be2-92ea-5c55a2bb083e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310343} :answer-field/id #uuid "730b6612-68ec-5a87-b552-79b4f8e997cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c20480ee-ea99-549e-9491-8b31f9d343e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337343" :question/problem "Consider a game of chance with $5$ prizes ($$0$, $$2$, $$3$, $$5$, and $$10$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$0$ | $10%$ |\n| $$2$ | $25%$ |\n| $$3$ | $30%$ |\n| $$5$ | $20%$ |\n| $$10$ | $15%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game, in dollars. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table.  If $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =0\\cdot f(0)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{10})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{3}{10})+5(\\frac{1}{5})+10(\\frac{3}{20}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{10}+1+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{10}{10}+\\frac{15}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{10} \\\\  & =3.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$\\,3.9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6428a164-c938-53fd-a798-489e69b4664b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309518} {:answer/id #uuid "68c27508-8311-5ccb-bb6b-7992734a314d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309554} {:answer/id #uuid "72909054-86b1-55c3-ae04-90260676f0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309638} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f735b1-f36c-5b7e-bab3-835deda869de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310178} {:answer/id #uuid "b135ef8d-469b-520e-90bc-a8909f816447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b135ef8d-469b-520e-90bc-a8909f816447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310180} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a9c31d-9039-594a-9b71-5c56b88011c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ca629ac-6f7f-5259-a58f-46e9b66ddd37" :question/math-academy-id "q-337348" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1568a632a02f644f7ef8c39417bb06973f0862494aa0c3c624880a87c5620ee.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +4\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{10}{41},\\beta =\\frac{8}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{41})}^{2}}≈0.31,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58adda3e-8028-5e76-bac2-ea8d2adff883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309419} {:answer/id #uuid "84c1fd0a-edd8-5f8d-960b-5bd0b1aa9f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309781} {:answer/id #uuid "93f6a0e5-5eb0-5e91-b456-c436f9c98ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309933} {:answer/id #uuid "df029acf-dafd-5fac-8a65-d98666644352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310539} {:answer/id #uuid "f14c158e-5a6b-5687-a604-b5e1428e81a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f6a0e5-5eb0-5e91-b456-c436f9c98ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309933} :answer-field/id #uuid "692a3a08-3e1f-5dc1-bbb1-d93c94619d1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dcaf4a1-d004-5d72-aec5-9cbcc1877083" :question/math-academy-id "q-337349" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bf264310dba0dc66332b3c8e0f2e1c780ca199e7d5ea3c1f21f5132a02e06dd.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{200})}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +8\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +6\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{9}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{9}{50})}^{2}}=0.3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31fe908a-f3d4-5a6a-855e-5b70bb23317e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309125} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddb2263-bba6-5ff9-8bb5-bd9ec6ace83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309218} {:answer/id #uuid "4b32b723-2a23-5499-89ec-83ec3ca742cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309320} {:answer/id #uuid "d5eae615-0624-5004-b90f-0e466ea659cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310470} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f4180c-c71c-528b-89f8-ef76386d11d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31fe908a-f3d4-5a6a-855e-5b70bb23317e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309125} :answer-field/id #uuid "00b72104-4ca0-51c8-b55f-e63d0bb54f37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "637f26cc-d79d-5280-a3ea-3093ba13a449" :question/math-academy-id "q-337350" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0841cfa21b90ba01207e42970b2f7e39c63c1df8c2b2dbd5154ae723ac5aa7a.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $18\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{72})}=72q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+72q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{8}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{25})}^{2}}=0.4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25eb9e4e-7615-53d1-b5ee-f7ded6a68829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309030} {:answer/id #uuid "26b8008c-a3dd-5994-bbc6-116c82fa58f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309039} {:answer/id #uuid "52e47ff3-f7b2-5c9b-a08a-14ac20762c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309376} {:answer/id #uuid "cc773859-459d-554e-b8e0-100468dfafb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310386} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4fd423-440a-5581-b6ee-f554672059eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26b8008c-a3dd-5994-bbc6-116c82fa58f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309039} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c33b536-df02-5068-8277-ec36a3a78df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4f9f944-9c0e-59c6-9fbc-f5fd024e3d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-337351" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e37bc2504bb5c95ef5624760ac1b4639c0ff02aeb79bd5dccb1e7257215e3b3c.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt},\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{200})}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +10\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 10\\alpha +2\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{26},\\beta =\\frac{1}{26}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{26})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{26})}^{2}}≈0.20,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea0ea24-6dfa-5652-8659-3fc0b2f343de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308980} {:answer/id #uuid "4092ebda-d278-51fa-b385-9f1466d4201c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309239} {:answer/id #uuid "5020603e-7949-52cc-bb47-893c8612b4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309356} {:answer/id #uuid "646e141a-63b0-55e8-8f7a-8813ea91a393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309520} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cf6947-7609-509a-8a6b-bf0398e09930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "646e141a-63b0-55e8-8f7a-8813ea91a393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309520} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed097df-26f6-5219-a41e-479d16650421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653862b9-5ee4-5517-b3d1-d992fa97dab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337352" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1933db5df10c1a3faf07f1d459a7736be682a3d01ab85e48214da21e7a9d1d5.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +5\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{16}{41},\\beta =\\frac{20}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{16}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{20}{41})}^{2}}≈0.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "306e4b1b-1f51-589b-a192-0d6a50126a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309115} {:answer/id #uuid "6d75ba25-0626-5cd9-94c2-dff2f6833ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309594} {:answer/id #uuid "bcaf3252-e283-5c88-8362-360136d8ea6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310276} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb390f2-653e-5240-af81-8a55d3e5ddd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcaf3252-e283-5c88-8362-360136d8ea6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310276} :answer-field/id #uuid "283b8846-366b-550b-b657-637bf0500c35" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aac8a2b-7d59-5439-9907-eb3ed3be090f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309065} {:answer/id #uuid "863b0ca2-e1db-5169-b922-74f9a137c606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309800} {:answer/id #uuid "963062b4-feaf-51ae-a386-faeafc793d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309950} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d5df4b-0b88-5acd-be76-ace956215530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310235} {:answer/id #uuid "e645f7b5-2488-5fd9-8803-44781509bb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aac8a2b-7d59-5439-9907-eb3ed3be090f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309065} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7957466-6fad-56bc-bf76-9acd442f566f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c07216-fde8-5eda-9d6d-38f89add3842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309515} {:answer/id #uuid "773505e4-b6d5-5d38-b849-ccf307a758f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309681} {:answer/id #uuid "77ac02c5-fa62-5aee-a2c3-e2464e54c382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{4}x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309686} {:answer/id #uuid "c5899af6-1799-5971-aa28-989fc6bed668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63c07216-fde8-5eda-9d6d-38f89add3842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309515} :answer-field/id #uuid "e074f782-cb1d-5110-b97e-876438227b03" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c6334e0-5953-55d2-849b-0ecb549465e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337510" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =12\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =6+6{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6+6{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6+6{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18284b2b-4baa-542f-b3e9-f9b101400186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308940} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff5d425-2d91-50b8-9bcc-9692165610b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308993} {:answer/id #uuid "53b8cc38-caf9-50a1-a118-c87e677f7809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} {:answer/id #uuid "68df02b9-4559-5126-84a0-ba28cb02fc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309555} {:answer/id #uuid "ac205e92-9455-5d91-bfba-6e02ad69a22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b8cc38-caf9-50a1-a118-c87e677f7809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} :answer-field/id #uuid "464e3dff-e0cc-5e85-ad7a-58ca73b13764" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554c57fc-e876-56b7-94d6-d7df7bf38bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309392} {:answer/id #uuid "597f39be-9a1f-5d38-9861-17c41bcc4e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309422} {:answer/id #uuid "5e975f0c-6327-5a3e-9a4a-3ba400131efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309456} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe953db-5211-5d7f-8fac-fc01e86f8803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554c57fc-e876-56b7-94d6-d7df7bf38bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309392} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7d836f-6d18-5bf8-a30a-8cd709e799d5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149d4e7b-e129-51ce-9d5c-05e86d24d773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308902} {:answer/id #uuid "8e85b76c-5f96-59bb-a728-d1a1b2826e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309882} {:answer/id #uuid "a8782715-ec59-53ed-8453-10ff273baec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310107} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a9ec6-48f6-5fda-9b04-48714a713242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a9ec6-48f6-5fda-9b04-48714a713242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6871c91-599d-5c8a-9961-f82fb77091f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a060a8-89ee-5d43-a547-481f4dd7a9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-337518" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =8{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =4-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4-4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =4x+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f17ea6-2ff3-5132-975e-362cf4a649f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308893} {:answer/id #uuid "491c7407-1990-5b9f-9631-5fae92efe638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309308} {:answer/id #uuid "996c9c61-bf13-55b7-9b1a-b9675cee9943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309983} {:answer/id #uuid "b0992b52-98b1-514c-a05a-ab4a1b306cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{15x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310173} {:answer/id #uuid "f545e20a-ba77-50dc-998a-718334cfde8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13f17ea6-2ff3-5132-975e-362cf4a649f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308893} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e0b39f-caa4-58c1-8675-b1061ca66bee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310817} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e8ad51-9c9e-58fe-a872-6f16f89b14f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}+x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308750} {:answer/id #uuid "0291f2ab-25e8-5bab-8297-30f63a454d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308764} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6a8bd0-b176-5b28-83c7-7edb6cfb5312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x{e}^{5x}+2{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309573} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c9fff4-5be4-57ed-8cb8-444cfbe7c58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}+2x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0291f2ab-25e8-5bab-8297-30f63a454d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308764} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4d4b3b-fa00-559c-8270-40b052ffa5bc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310872} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a714dd8-21a7-58bf-958d-041dc5c8bdb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308824} {:answer/id #uuid "a3182f63-c2b9-500d-8ee4-04af5041269d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310064} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cc27e7-4dd9-5347-be40-db1499ebd2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x+{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310086} {:answer/id #uuid "e3197ab5-7677-5dd0-8dfc-4a6a0f587ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x+2{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5cc27e7-4dd9-5347-be40-db1499ebd2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x+{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310086} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4201788-6702-5898-8b36-c1c496ce7041" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75f02fdb-eb93-5139-a287-32c95146a943" :question/math-academy-id "q-337524" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =20{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x=\\frac{{e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =20{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\  & =20{e}^{-5x}(\\frac{{e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}}{2}) \\\\  & =10{e}^{-5x}({e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =10-10{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =10-10{e}^{-10x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 10-10{e}^{-10x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =10x+{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =10x{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e5abb6f-5e08-56e9-914b-9e552004de67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309343} {:answer/id #uuid "9c061ea9-9f13-52cb-98a8-ba50801a69d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310009} {:answer/id #uuid "ab28e6b5-91dd-5722-bf96-b9c08de9b1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310133} {:answer/id #uuid "b24de0d4-ef39-5e91-8eb7-4502575f46de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310189} {:answer/id #uuid "dde19e16-0392-5e2e-ade5-eca93831d4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde19e16-0392-5e2e-ade5-eca93831d4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310527} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc6fe2c-ab12-5155-b3c2-977ab6f2f2fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6341a1d8-e09a-59b9-84dc-d4331496dbcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-337547" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x})-(2{e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot x{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)({e}^{2x})+(-\\frac{1}{x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1) \\\\  & =(A-1){e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-1)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}\\underbrace{\\,-\\,{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49cfa1a5-d955-5830-8e0f-67dba30d0ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309312} {:answer/id #uuid "697c3eac-9905-5459-b6f5-4386fe61c9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309565} {:answer/id #uuid "77cc720c-1b84-519a-8d85-b5fb90614e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309688} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8951e1-1553-59dc-b7ed-9ee06f0591da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309883} {:answer/id #uuid "dec14dcd-f50b-57c7-a3f9-c7865ab0e38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77cc720c-1b84-519a-8d85-b5fb90614e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309688} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1074fc-b51f-50f9-a46d-dbca7d5b9a33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b3e0e9-58f0-5e42-bcb7-8d7fbdbe1863" :question/math-academy-id "q-337548" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})-(-{e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot x{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{-x}+(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})x{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+\\underbrace{\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd435dd-0d23-523a-b22c-021099293641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309078} {:answer/id #uuid "5d113c1b-eeac-582b-adfc-27b973e4b4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309446} {:answer/id #uuid "625ce389-3726-5627-b3ee-ab0bc649071e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309495} {:answer/id #uuid "74ff317f-1066-599e-939c-5940ef563f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309659} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7f960d-6940-5e33-a0f0-24a8fdfaa2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74ff317f-1066-599e-939c-5940ef563f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309659} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec673ff-c866-5730-b940-d8c2bc43cd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85065fe3-60b8-561d-a678-99a217a32ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337570" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/98/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a43b3012-16cb-5208-bab4-c20770b3f2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-2}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310071} {:answer/id #uuid "db134198-ef62-58eb-9844-bd15199bb96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310504} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e7e8f3-2ca3-5b4e-a232-4037add6e168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db134198-ef62-58eb-9844-bd15199bb96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310504} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b62e41-1376-596c