} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ad52a0-151c-5757-97bd-153874440b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310217} {:answer/id #uuid "cb298530-774e-57d8-9ccd-9f5c75f7852f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-h(y))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310375} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a49e34-5fd3-5cc8-9dfd-3fdc3f06520f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{exp}⁡(h(y))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ad52a0-151c-5757-97bd-153874440b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310217} :answer-field/id #uuid "93da18b2-0f04-5d92-8287-4db5128efec2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f705cdc-41c2-5f9a-8445-edd919c31cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-2}{e}^{1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309228} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0481b1-b497-58f4-b105-3f3d55abce0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309878} {:answer/id #uuid "fa34ecd1-5439-525f-9135-2393396f1316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa34ecd1-5439-525f-9135-2393396f1316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310741} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c8a9a3-d29e-566e-98ae-8919fefcf054" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311447} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e90167-98bc-5494-9877-95fbe03661c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308795} {:answer/id #uuid "062343fa-a7e4-5446-adf9-0e582f17946e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308798} {:answer/id #uuid "26a3a9dd-860b-5c80-af07-44e21596f9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309037} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6a1317-f7f4-5800-95ee-65866a9a09b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05e90167-98bc-5494-9877-95fbe03661c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308795} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62f2e9c-b823-5d73-8121-225ae6240239" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43a2c42c-804d-5119-bec4-269847a33364" :question/math-academy-id "q-337619" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{3})=6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xy)=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}-(-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{1}{2y})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\frac{1}{2y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}(2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055e1fc8-915a-592f-849d-e505bc5d7841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=12+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308786} {:answer/id #uuid "075f7d5c-29bb-5c01-9e06-079d3b73be05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=12-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308802} {:answer/id #uuid "076651f6-c3a1-5d95-b6eb-5ad4ea6151e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=9-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308804} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fdeba2-9381-5542-8f53-6c63631fe3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=12-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310344} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6bc4fb-eefa-5060-9137-7ddd78466ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "075f7d5c-29bb-5c01-9e06-079d3b73be05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=12-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308802} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f0696b-8f7a-5234-be84-d44c7b4f12b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1473c225-873e-51f5-9bb2-6f7541afad6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337654" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(3+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3}) & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, let $u=\\frac{t}{3}.$ Then $t=3u$ and $\\text{d}t=3\\,\\text{d}u,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (3u){e}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9\\int u{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9(u-1){e}^{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back $u=\\frac{t}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =9(\\frac{t}{3}-1){e}^{t/3} \\\\  & =(3t-9){e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(3t-9){e}^{t/3}+C \\\\  & =(-4t+12){e}^{t/3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx=-4t+12+C{e}^{-t/3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+12-4t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=12-4t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43db9c4f-09ac-5726-abb0-9c3e10295f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309264} {:answer/id #uuid "53fc2dd4-6677-53b0-8afa-2eaf1f098593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309386} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98b50f-aa8f-56a7-ae48-c3e43c87eb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309704} {:answer/id #uuid "8628dabe-0b8d-579d-8578-57c1c49dd7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309797} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e2b971-981c-5a94-aaf6-a5af90c69723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43db9c4f-09ac-5726-abb0-9c3e10295f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309264} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a52627-2e7d-50f9-b71f-c481a2332973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59e9cb29-7d37-5d4c-9754-fdc1e96d8fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337683" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t=t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{2}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|}=(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =1-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =1-\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad280c2-db33-5b17-8ea2-ac18b43898e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=5-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309197} {:answer/id #uuid "6323f61a-bb37-510a-aa62-63053e089258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309505} {:answer/id #uuid "88e42f9a-67a2-580a-b86f-cdef663d7ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309824} {:answer/id #uuid "a614d826-7900-576d-a893-62dc32ac2a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=3-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310088} {:answer/id #uuid "e073e492-94b9-5f73-aee1-96e918e47740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e073e492-94b9-5f73-aee1-96e918e47740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310545} :answer-field/id #uuid "f42c0423-10e1-55a2-bb37-ebd1a4704761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f34ae243-5d21-54e5-a082-840e708d60ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-337699" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =(1-3t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =(1-3t){e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t-3\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & ={e}^{t}-3(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =4-3t+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+4-3t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=4-3t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e617450-43e8-5abe-b711-55c66f7eedaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308846} {:answer/id #uuid "1e242e11-fc07-5c22-ba05-9be291d6372f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308976} {:answer/id #uuid "7a739002-c8a3-5f62-8e33-a3e161ee65d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309701} {:answer/id #uuid "8df8861d-53ee-51cb-b585-59e91d5a65fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309877} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bf282c-c85d-5b89-90a8-45f21c8457f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8bf282c-c85d-5b89-90a8-45f21c8457f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310358} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3109ada-d6b9-5a56-b743-64fc5cb5f0b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5583cd7-45bf-57f0-9c2b-4cb14407a01b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337713" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then the general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{3(1-t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{3(1-t)}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{3(1-t)}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (\\frac{3}{t}-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+3t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}+3t.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=3t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}+3t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b93f245-065a-5185-93d4-362871323a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309436} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf62c93-3c68-5005-a0a5-44ce1102a4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309860} {:answer/id #uuid "9feccca2-418c-54e0-b9e3-670e953d7f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310040} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a54200-1ebf-508f-8cc9-818acba425db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310200} {:answer/id #uuid "ca259ff8-ab57-573c-8822-ba8b0ebe9797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b93f245-065a-5185-93d4-362871323a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b337c6e-8a90-5309-a7b0-a728b817c334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1184c7b6-6662-5793-8da6-caaadcf27409" :question/math-academy-id "q-337721" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{(1-t)(2-t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t) \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-3+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\text{ }g(t)=-2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0453cd41-d901-5009-8f65-b4cfa22bbf3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+t-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308776} {:answer/id #uuid "968fb4e3-ef61-5e36-9a5e-745bf0584f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-t-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309955} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ac9c38-fc14-5221-bccf-d2099caf9760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310234} {:answer/id #uuid "cb999aec-912d-5ca1-8862-8e6e0ddbe6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}-t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310379} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fff5b2-a814-58fe-916f-2b7cf8737f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9fff5b2-a814-58fe-916f-2b7cf8737f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310740} :answer-field/id #uuid "65612883-612c-5a33-92a8-cd25d69a5a58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06aec42c-90c8-5e5c-a056-f5d209f3a8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-337727" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2)-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x=t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t=(2t-4){e}^{t/2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x=t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{t/2} & =t{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/2}) & =t{e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int t{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t/2} & =(2t-4){e}^{t/2}+C \\\\ x & =2t-4+C{e}^{-t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x(t+2)-{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4)(t+2)-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=t+2,\\text{ }g(t)=-{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=-{t}^{2}+t+2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ae1e0e-3214-5887-a729-309c961fbd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310120} {:answer/id #uuid "b93668a2-9df7-55ae-9946-dfcbafb7cde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ae1e0e-3214-5887-a729-309c961fbd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310120} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5496f6-3e7a-5617-973a-4c86197e42f4" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64892727-99b0-560e-98f0-30da810430ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309521} {:answer/id #uuid "6c79d0a6-f429-5985-8082-c52185e0bdad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answ