db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309186} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d0fa66-65bb-55c2-b1d1-a4eb9b43199c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3997fb81-d70b-5b9b-bca3-623b90b84aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309186} :answer-field/id #uuid "deec0c55-c23d-5ed6-9f01-5868ac0b477e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c34cbae-eabd-5c38-b4a3-0b29397c07e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337739" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}))}^{2}+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2ab\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & +a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot (2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{y}_{1}]+b[{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c1ce28-8bfe-5b09-a15d-7c4b76ecfa92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309490} {:answer/id #uuid "92b108b0-3b47-556c-89ac-daa66d2c979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} {:answer/id #uuid "a553f444-e68c-53b4-8d93-575a4cc7cf15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310080} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fbe008-7f1a-5a31-9cb7-f9dd2c67fa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310318} {:answer/id #uuid "ec46c1a1-e0b3-5094-a3be-1a97aa9a9b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92b108b0-3b47-556c-89ac-daa66d2c979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1bdc7f-51da-5393-bc6a-4b97c1c1ced8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a1235f2-6c7a-5f14-b04f-a38d354a3d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337837" :question/problem "Given that $D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=-2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0087b403-c7ca-5331-b8d9-439842b47d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308747} {:answer/id #uuid "2318a1d4-07cb-5825-b016-5a6bedb71369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309013} {:answer/id #uuid "24eaf44d-47ed-5093-8aac-20086979fb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309025} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfbec37-9f6d-5505-8a86-50fe7b1a3f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310272} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dfd225-ae4e-51ac-b1b7-2a7e89f67ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2318a1d4-07cb-5825-b016-5a6bedb71369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309013} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb1b820-e722-50f0-8bee-2ae6008feb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46fe989c-8dfc-559c-bcd3-bca514dce992" :question/math-academy-id "q-337843" :question/problem "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-3,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=2x,$ and ${f}_{0}=-3.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-3)\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+2x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-\\frac{3}{x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{3}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
